    "created_at": "2022-10-16T18:52:03Z",
    "updated_at": "2022-10-16T18:52:03Z",
    "body": "I would really love to have this feature. I am used to having my branch background color red if dirty or yellow if in a modified state (among other color/logic). I don't typically care what the counts of each status type are, and I don't really like the aesthetics of the symbols for file status/counts.\r\n\r\nI haven't had time to thoroughly read through the source code but it appears the [git_status module](https://github.com/starship/starship/blob/master/src/modules/git_status.rs#L203) relies on parsing `git status --porcelain`, so any similar implementation for git_branch module would be redundant.\r\n\r\nOne off the cuff idea would be to simply expose the git_branch name as a variable in the git_status module so it can be used in a config format string under `[git_status]` section, although that would create a dependency across modules. I'm not sure how the config variables work, but `$branch` and `$remote_branch` could just be empty strings if the user does not opt-in to the git_branch module. That way they aren't paying for extra computations they don't want to use, and users who want it can reuse the result of another module they already opted in for.\r\n\r\nA hacky workaround might be leveraging `starship_precmd_user_func` to set an environment variable with an ANSI color escape sequence based on the output of `git status --porcelain` (yes, this is completely redundant to git_status). You could then print that prior to git_branch using the env_var module -- e.g., `$env_var$git_branch`. I'm not sure how well this would work, since I don't know if characters are sanitized/escaped when formatting them, and I'm not sure how colors are formatted between modules (e.g., if reset is printed between modules or something).",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
